> Pooling and repetition: a hand-checkable adjoint

For a4×4 matrix filled row-wise with1,…,16, a2×2 mean pool is
[[3.5,5.5],[11.5,13.5]]. Nearest repetition copies each pooled value into a2×2 block; it
cannot recover the lost within-block differences. If T is repetition after mean pooling,
T is an orthogonal projection onto block-constant images: T²=T and Tᵀ=T. Thus for
J(X)=0.5||T(X)||², its gradient is TᵀT(X)=T(X), not four times or one quarter of it.
Pooling's adjoint copies the incoming block adjoint divided by4; repetition's adjoint sums
its four replicas. The executed figure shows X,T(X),dJ/dX on the same fixed scale.

A tiny4×4 VAE with one latent, channel widths2/1 and all84 learned scalars set to zero has
mu=0,log variance=0 and KL=0. With any explicit finite epsilon, its zero-weight decoder gives
16 zero logits, probabilities0.5 and binary-target NLL16log2≈11.09035. No training or random
sampling is hidden in either check.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/conv_vae_hand.png", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n              = dm::nn;
  const auto          type = n::DType::Float64;
  std::vector<double> values(16);
  std::iota(values.begin(), values.end(), 1.);
  auto       x      = n::from_vector(values, {1, 4, 4, 1}).requires_grad_();
  const auto pooled = n::avg_pool2d(x, 2, 2), repeated = n::upsample_nearest(pooled, 2);
  const auto derivative = n::grad(.5 * n::sum(n::square(repeated)), {x})[0];
  if (pooled.to_vector<double>() != std::vector<double>{3.5, 5.5, 11.5, 13.5} ||
      derivative.to_vector<double>() != repeated.to_vector<double>())
    return 1;
  n::save_image_grid(
      n::concat({x / 16, repeated / 16, derivative / 16}, 0),
      "build/conv_vae_hand.png",
      3,
      0,
      1,
      4,
      220. / 255,
      32);
  n::ParameterStore p(2011, type);
  const auto binary = n::cast(n::greater_equal(x / 16, .5), type), epsilon = n::ones({1, 1}, type);
  n::models::ConvolutionalVariationalAutoencoder(p, binary, epsilon, 1, 2, 1);
  for (const auto& name : p.names())
    p.set(name, n::zeros(p.at(name).shape(), type));
  const auto o = n::models::ConvolutionalVariationalAutoencoder(p, binary, epsilon, 1, 2, 1);
  if (p.count() != 84 || o.kl.item() != 0 || std::abs(o.loss.item() - 16 * std::log(2.)) > 1e-12 ||
      n::min(o.probability).item() != .5 || n::max(o.probability).item() != .5)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "Pooled values {}; adjoint exactly equals repeated means. Zero-model loss {:.9f}, KL0, all16 "
      "probabilities0.5.\n",
      pooled.values_str(),
      o.loss.item());
}
```
